## std/memoria.pengu — manual heap memory management.
##
## `memoria` expone las primitivas canónicas para reservar, redimensionar,
## copiar y manipular bloques de memoria del heap en PenguScript. Cada método se
## declara `shard T`, así que se monomorfiza por tipo concreto en tiempo de
## compilación: no hay boxing, ni vtables, ni sobrecoste en runtime. `alloc of
## int` y `alloc of Player` son dos funciones C distintas, cada una con su
## `sizeof` ya resuelto.
##
## ## Lo que **no** está aquí
## `memoria` **no** expone `free`, `sizeof` ni `address_of`: esos ya viven en
## el lenguaje y duplicarlos sería anti-Pengunic. La forma canónica de cada
## concepto es:
##
## | Concepto | Forma canónica |
## |---|---|
## | Liberar | `banish p` / `defer banish p` / `errdefer banish p` |
## | Tamaño | `size of T` (operador) |
## | Dirección | `sigil of x` (operador) |
## | Desreferencia | `essence of p` (operador) |
## | Destructor de rune | `derive Nexus` + `banish (essence of p)` |
##
## ## Zeroed por defecto
## `alloc` y `alloc_array` devuelven memoria **puesta a cero**. No existe
## `alloc_uninit`: un bloque sin inicializar es una deuda de seguridad, no una
## primitiva de biblioteca. Si de verdad necesitas uno (rendimiento medido,
## código de arranque), declara un bloque `antiquus` con
## `#include <stdlib.h>` y llama a `malloc` directamente, documentando el
## motivo en el punto de uso. La biblioteca estándar no esconderá esa deuda por
## ti.
##
## ## OOM: aborta por defecto, `maybe` con `try_*`
## Si el sistema se queda sin memoria, `alloc`, `alloc_array` y `resize_array`
## abortan con `[PENGU] out of memory` (la política `PENGU_OOM_ABORT` del
## runtime, ver LANGUAGE.md §5.0). Si prefieres manejar el fallo, usa
## `try_alloc`, `try_alloc_array` o `try_resize_array`, que devuelven
## `maybe none` en lugar de abortar. En `try_resize_array`, un fallo deja el
## bloque original **válido y en propiedad del llamador**.
##
## ## Composición con el lenguaje
## La pareja canónica es `memoria.alloc of T` + `banish`:
##
## ```pengu
## import std.memoria
##
## rune Player:
##     hp as int
##
## weave main into int:
##     var p as ref to Player is calling memoria.alloc of Player
##     defer banish p
##     set p->hp is 100
##     return 0
## ```
##
## Para runes con `derive Nexus`, `banish p` libera el bloque pero **no**
## invoca el destructor: llama a `banish (essence of p)` antes de `banish p`,
## o envuelve la liberación en un helper.
##
## ## Memoria
## **Todos** los métodos de este módulo devuelven punteros que el llamador debe
## liberar con `banish`. No hay gestión automática: el heap manual es manual.
##
## ## Ejemplo: buffer dinámico
## ```pengu
## import std.memoria
##
## weave main into int:
##     var cap as usize is 8
##     var buf as ref to int is calling memoria.alloc_array of int with cap
##     defer banish buf
##     calling memoria.fill of int with buf, 42, cap
##     set buf is calling memoria.resize_array of int with buf, cap, 16
##     set cap is 16
##     return 0
## ```
##
## `defer banish buf` nombra la **variable**, así que libera el puntero
## vigente al salir del scope (el que devolvió `resize_array`), no una copia
## del valor inicial. No hay doble `banish`.

include "string.h"

import std.ward

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module Version (LANGUAGE.md §19.0: every hand-written std module exports one)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Memoria module version.
const MEMORIA_VERSION as string is "2.12.0"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime declarations
#
# `pengu_memoria_*` are additive runtime helpers (pengu_runtime.{h,c}) that own
# the alloc/realloc/OOM policy. `memmove`/`memset`/`memcmp` come straight from
# <string.h>: they are byte-level and identical for every `T`, so a dedicated
# runtime function would only add indirection.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Reserva `n` bytes a cero; aborta según `PENGU_OOM_ABORT`.
declare pengu_memoria_alloc             with n as usize into ref to void
## Reserva `count` elementos de `elem_size` bytes a cero; aborta según OOM.
declare pengu_memoria_alloc_array       with count as usize, elem_size as usize into ref to void
## Como `pengu_memoria_alloc` pero devuelve NULL en OOM.
declare pengu_memoria_try_alloc         with n as usize into ref to void
## Como `pengu_memoria_alloc_array` pero devuelve NULL en OOM.
declare pengu_memoria_try_alloc_array   with count as usize, elem_size as usize into ref to void
## Redimensiona preservando `min(old_n, new_n)`; la cola nueva va a cero.
declare pengu_memoria_resize            with p as ref to void, old_n as usize, new_n as usize, elem_size as usize into ref to void
## Como `pengu_memoria_resize` pero devuelve NULL en OOM dejando `p` válido.
declare pengu_memoria_try_resize        with p as ref to void, old_n as usize, new_n as usize, elem_size as usize into ref to void
## Intercambio byte a byte de `elem_size` bytes.
declare pengu_memoria_swap              with a as ref to void, b as ref to void, elem_size as usize into void
## Relleno de `count` elementos con el patrón de `elem_size` bytes en `value`.
declare pengu_memoria_fill              with dst as ref to void, value as ref to frozen void, count as usize, elem_size as usize into void

## `memmove` de <string.h>: copia sin solapar ya soportado.
declare memmove with dst as ref to void, src as ref to frozen void, n as usize into void
## `memset` de <string.h>: relleno de bytes.
declare memset  with dst as ref to void, value as int, n as usize into void
## `memcmp` de <string.h>: comparación de bytes.
declare memcmp  with a as ref to frozen void, b as ref to frozen void, n as usize into int

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Alocación (4)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Reserva un valor de tipo `T` en el heap, **inicializado a cero**.
##
## ## Memoria
## El llamador debe liberar el resultado con `banish` (o `defer banish`).
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var p as ref to Player is calling memoria.alloc of Player
## defer banish p
## ```
weave alloc shard T into ref to T:
    return calling pengu_memoria_alloc with (size of T)


## Reserva un array de `n` elementos de tipo `T`, **inicializado a cero**.
##
## ## Precondiciones
## `n > 0`. Con `n == 0` el comportamiento es el de `calloc(1, 0)`, que la
## norma C deja a discreción de la implementación (NULL o un bloque mínimo);
## no lo uses como caso normal.
##
## ## Memoria
## El llamador debe liberar el resultado con `banish`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var buf as ref to int is calling memoria.alloc_array of int with 100
## defer banish buf
## ```
weave alloc_array shard T with n as usize into ref to T:
    return calling pengu_memoria_alloc_array with n, (size of T)


## Como `alloc`, pero devuelve `maybe none` en caso de OOM en lugar de abortar.
##
## ## Memoria
## Hacen falta **dos** liberaciones, porque un `maybe ref to T` no es dueño del
## bloque apuntado: la caja contiene una copia del puntero, no su propiedad.
## 1. `banish p` libera el bloque que devolvió el runtime;
## 2. `banish mp` libera la caja del `maybe`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var mp as maybe ref to int is calling memoria.try_alloc of int
## if p as ref to int is mp:
##     set essence of p is 1
##     banish p
## banish mp
## ```
weave try_alloc shard T into maybe ref to T:
    let p as ref to void is calling pengu_memoria_try_alloc with (size of T)
    if p == null:
        return maybe none
    return some p


## Como `alloc_array`, pero devuelve `maybe none` en caso de OOM.
##
## ## Memoria
## Como en `try_alloc`: `banish p` libera el bloque y `banish mp` la caja.
weave try_alloc_array shard T with n as usize into maybe ref to T:
    let p as ref to void is calling pengu_memoria_try_alloc_array with n, (size of T)
    if p == null:
        return maybe none
    return some p


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Redimensionado (2)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Redimensiona un bloque previamente asignado con `alloc_array`/`resize_array`.
##
## ## Por qué pide `old_n`
## `realloc` de C no sabe cuántos elementos hay dentro del bloque. El llamador
## tiene que saberlo igualmente, y olvidarlo es un bug silencioso que corrompe
## la cola nueva. Al pedir `old_n` el contrato queda explícito y el runtime
## puede preservar exactamente `min(old_n, new_n)` elementos y poner a cero el
## resto.
##
## ## Semántica
## - `new_n > old_n`: crece; contenido preservado, cola **zeroed**.
## - `new_n < old_n`: encoge; primeros `new_n` elementos preservados.
## - `new_n == old_n`: no-op; devuelve `p` **sin** liberarlo.
## - `p == null`: equivale a `alloc_array of T with new_n`.
## - `new_n == 0`: no soportado (igual que `realloc(p, 0)` en C: indefinido).
##
## ## Precondiciones
## `p` debe provenir de `alloc_array`/`resize_array` sobre el mismo `T`, y
## `old_n` debe ser el tamaño **actual** del bloque.
##
## ## Memoria
## ⚠️ Tras una llamada con éxito, el puntero antiguo **queda inválido**: usa
## solo el retorno. `realloc` libera el bloque viejo si tiene que moverlo, y si
## no lo mueve el retorno es el mismo puntero. En ambos casos el patrón seguro
## es reasignar la misma variable y dejar que `defer banish` la lea al salir:
## ```pengu
## var buf as ref to byte is calling memoria.alloc_array of byte with 8
## defer banish buf
## set buf is calling memoria.resize_array of byte with buf, 8, 16
## # 'buf' ya apunta al bloque nuevo; el defer libera ESE puntero.
## ```
weave resize_array shard T with p as ref to T, old_n as usize, new_n as usize into ref to T:
    return calling pengu_memoria_resize with p, old_n, new_n, (size of T)


## Como `resize_array`, pero devuelve `maybe none` en caso de OOM.
##
## ## Memoria
## Si el resultado es `maybe none` y `p` era no nulo, el bloque original sigue
## siendo válido y propiedad del llamador. Si es `some np`, el bloque viejo
## queda inválido: libera el bloque con `banish np` y la caja con `banish mp`.
weave try_resize_array shard T with p as ref to T, old_n as usize, new_n as usize into maybe ref to T:
    let np as ref to void is calling pengu_memoria_try_resize with p, old_n, new_n, (size of T)
    if np == null:
        return maybe none
    return some np


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Operaciones bulk (5)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Copia `n` elementos de tipo `T` desde `src` a `dst`.
##
## ## Seguridad
## Usa `memmove` internamente: es seguro aunque los bloques se solapen. El
## coste extra frente a `memcpy` es despreciable (glibc detecta la ausencia de
## solape). `src` es `ref to frozen T`: `copy` nunca escribe en el origen.
##
## ## Precondiciones
## `dst` y `src` deben apuntar a bloques de al menos `n * (size of T)` bytes.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling memoria.copy of int with dst, src, 4
## ```
weave copy shard T with dst as ref to T, src as ref to frozen T, n as usize into void:
    calling memmove with dst, src, (n * (size of T))


## Rellena `n` elementos de `dst` con el valor `value`.
##
## A diferencia de `zero`, el patrón no tiene por qué ser cero (un `int`, un
## `float`, un puntero…). Se copia el patrón byte a byte, elemento a elemento.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling memoria.fill of int with buf, 0, 100      # equivalente a zero
## calling memoria.fill of int with buf, -1, 100
## calling memoria.fill of float with buf, 1.5, 50
## ```
weave fill shard T with dst as ref to T, value as T, n as usize into void:
    calling pengu_memoria_fill with dst, (sigil of value), n, (size of T)


## Pone a cero `n` elementos de tipo `T` en `dst`.
##
## ## Por qué está separado de `fill`
## Resetear un buffer es el patrón más común, y `memset` es más rápido que un
## bucle de `fill` que copia un patrón de varios bytes.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling memoria.zero of int with buf, 16
## ```
weave zero shard T with dst as ref to T, n as usize into void:
    calling memset with dst, 0, (n * (size of T))


## Intercambia los valores apuntados por `a` y `b` (byte a byte).
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var x as int is 1
## var y as int is 2
## calling memoria.swap of int with (sigil of x), (sigil of y)
## # x == 2, y == 1
## ```
weave swap shard T with a as ref to T, b as ref to T into void:
    calling pengu_memoria_swap with a, b, (size of T)


## Compara byte a byte `n` elementos de tipo `T` en `a` y `b`.
##
## ## Retorno
## - `-1` si `a` es menor (el primer byte distinto es menor en `a`),
## - `0` si son iguales,
## - `1` si `a` es mayor.
##
## ## Nota
## Es una comparación de bytes **sin signo**, como `memcmp`. No respeta el
## orden lógico del tipo (para eso usa los métodos de `Ordo` sobre valores).
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var n as int is calling memoria.compare of byte with a, b, 8
## if n < 0: ... # a < b
## ```
weave compare shard T with a as ref to T, b as ref to T, n as usize into int:
    let raw as int is calling memcmp with a, b, (n * (size of T))
    if raw < 0:
        return 0 - 1
    if raw > 0:
        return 1
    return 0


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Utilidad (1)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Devuelve cuántos bytes ocupan `n` elementos de tipo `T`.
##
## Equivale a `n * (size of T)`, pero como función sirve en contextos genéricos
## donde ya tienes un `T` y quieres el tamaño total del bloque.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## let total is calling memoria.bytes_for of Player with 100
## ```
weave bytes_for shard T with n as usize into usize:
    return n * (size of T)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Rune de apoyo para los tests: sin campos con dueño, `banish` libera directo.
rune MemoriaPoint:
    x as int
    y as int

## Helper de test: igualdad exacta de `int`.
weave _t_int with got as int, want as int into void:
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with got, want

## Helper de test: igualdad exacta de `usize` expresada como `int`.
weave _t_usize with got as usize, want as usize into void:
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (got to int), (want to int)

# ─── alloc ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "alloc returns a zeroed int":
    var p as ref to int is calling alloc of int
    calling _t_int with (essence of p), 0
    banish p

test "alloc supports writing and reading back":
    var p as ref to int is calling alloc of int
    set essence of p is 42
    calling _t_int with (essence of p), 42
    banish p

test "alloc of float is zeroed":
    var p as ref to float is calling alloc of float
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (essence of p), 0.0
    set essence of p is 1.5
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (essence of p), 1.5
    banish p

test "alloc of byte is zeroed":
    var p as ref to byte is calling alloc of byte
    calling _t_int with ((essence of p) to int), 0
    banish p

test "alloc of a rune is zeroed":
    var p as ref to MemoriaPoint is calling alloc of MemoriaPoint
    calling _t_int with p->x, 0
    calling _t_int with p->y, 0
    set p->x is 7
    set p->y is 9
    calling _t_int with p->x, 7
    calling _t_int with p->y, 9
    banish p

# ─── alloc_array ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "alloc_array of one element is zeroed":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 1
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 0
    banish buf

test "alloc_array of many elements is zeroed":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 100
    var i as int is 0
    var ok as bool is true
    while i < 100:
        if (buf at i) != 0:
            set ok is false
        set i is i + 1
    calling ward.assert_true with ok
    banish buf

test "alloc_array supports indexed writes":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 10
    set buf at 0 is 5
    set buf at 9 is 99
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 5
    calling _t_int with (buf at 9), 99
    calling _t_int with (buf at 5), 0
    banish buf

test "alloc_array of byte keeps every value":
    var buf as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 4
    set buf at 0 is 1
    set buf at 1 is 2
    set buf at 2 is 3
    set buf at 3 is 4
    calling _t_int with ((buf at 0) to int), 1
    calling _t_int with ((buf at 3) to int), 4
    banish buf

test "alloc_array of float is zeroed and writable":
    var buf as ref to float is calling alloc_array of float with 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 0), 0.0
    set buf at 2 is 2.5
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 2), 2.5
    banish buf

# ─── try_alloc / try_alloc_array ────────────────────────────────────────────

test "try_alloc returns some and a usable pointer":
    var mp as maybe ref to int is calling try_alloc of int
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with mp
    if p as ref to int is mp:
        set essence of p is 11
        calling _t_int with (essence of p), 11
        banish p
    banish mp

test "try_alloc_array returns some and zeroes":
    var mp as maybe ref to int is calling try_alloc_array of int with 4
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with mp
    if p as ref to int is mp:
        calling _t_int with (p at 0), 0
        set p at 3 is 7
        calling _t_int with (p at 3), 7
        banish p
    banish mp

test "try_alloc_array of one element":
    var mp as maybe ref to byte is calling try_alloc_array of byte with 1
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with mp
    if p as ref to byte is mp:
        set p at 0 is 200
        calling _t_int with ((p at 0) to int), 200
        banish p
    banish mp

# ─── resize_array ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "resize preserves the old content":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 4
    set buf at 0 is 10
    set buf at 1 is 20
    set buf at 2 is 30
    set buf at 3 is 40
    set buf is calling resize_array of int with buf, 4, 8
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 10
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 40
    banish buf

test "resize zeroes the new tail":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    set buf at 0 is 1
    set buf is calling resize_array of int with buf, 2, 6
    calling _t_int with (buf at 1), 0
    calling _t_int with (buf at 5), 0
    banish buf

test "resize shrink keeps the prefix":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 6
    set buf at 0 is 1
    set buf at 5 is 6
    set buf is calling resize_array of int with buf, 6, 2
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 1
    calling _t_int with (buf at 1), 0
    banish buf

test "resize with equal sizes keeps the content":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    set buf at 1 is 55
    set buf is calling resize_array of int with buf, 3, 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 1), 55
    banish buf

test "resize from null behaves like alloc_array":
    var null_buf as ref to int is null
    var buf as ref to int is calling resize_array of int with null_buf, 0, 4
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 0
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 0
    set buf at 3 is 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 3
    banish buf

test "resize grows a byte buffer":
    var buf as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 2
    set buf at 0 is 9
    set buf is calling resize_array of byte with buf, 2, 5
    calling _t_int with ((buf at 0) to int), 9
    calling _t_int with ((buf at 4) to int), 0
    banish buf

# ─── try_resize_array ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "try_resize returns some and preserves content":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    set buf at 0 is 8
    var mp as maybe ref to int is calling try_resize_array of int with buf, 3, 6
    if p as ref to int is mp:
        calling _t_int with (p at 0), 8
        calling _t_int with (p at 5), 0
        banish p
    else:
        banish buf
    banish mp

test "try_resize with equal sizes returns the same pointer":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    set buf at 0 is 4
    var mp as maybe ref to int is calling try_resize_array of int with buf, 2, 2
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with mp
    if p as ref to int is mp:
        calling _t_int with (p at 0), 4
        banish p
    banish mp

test "try_resize from null allocates zeroed":
    var null_buf as ref to int is null
    var mp as maybe ref to int is calling try_resize_array of int with null_buf, 0, 3
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with mp
    if p as ref to int is mp:
        calling _t_int with (p at 2), 0
        banish p
    banish mp

# ─── copy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "copy moves non-overlapping blocks":
    var src as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    set src at 0 is 1
    set src at 1 is 2
    set src at 2 is 3
    var dst as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    calling copy of int with dst, src, 3
    calling _t_int with (dst at 0), 1
    calling _t_int with (dst at 2), 3
    banish src
    banish dst

test "copy overlaps forward safely":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 5
    set buf at 0 is 1
    set buf at 1 is 2
    set buf at 2 is 3
    set buf at 3 is 4
    set buf at 4 is 5
    calling copy of int with (sigil of (buf at 1)), (sigil of (buf at 0)), 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 1), 1
    calling _t_int with (buf at 2), 2
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 4), 5
    banish buf

test "copy overlaps backward safely":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 5
    set buf at 0 is 1
    set buf at 1 is 2
    set buf at 2 is 3
    set buf at 3 is 4
    set buf at 4 is 5
    calling copy of int with (sigil of (buf at 0)), (sigil of (buf at 1)), 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 2
    calling _t_int with (buf at 1), 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 2), 4
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 4
    banish buf

test "copy of zero elements is a no-op":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    set b at 0 is 5
    calling copy of int with a, b, 0
    calling _t_int with (a at 0), 0
    banish a
    banish b

test "copy works on byte buffers":
    var src as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 2
    set src at 0 is 7
    set src at 1 is 8
    var dst as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 2
    calling copy of byte with dst, src, 2
    calling _t_int with ((dst at 0) to int), 7
    calling _t_int with ((dst at 1) to int), 8
    banish src
    banish dst

# ─── fill ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "fill writes a non-zero int pattern":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 4
    calling fill of int with buf, -1, 4
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 0 - 1
    calling _t_int with (buf at 3), 0 - 1
    banish buf

test "fill writes a float pattern":
    var buf as ref to float is calling alloc_array of float with 3
    calling fill of float with buf, 1.5, 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 0), 1.5
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 2), 1.5
    banish buf

test "fill with zero matches zero":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    set buf at 0 is 9
    calling fill of int with buf, 0, 3
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 0
    banish buf

test "fill works on a single element":
    var buf as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 1
    calling fill of byte with buf, 255, 1
    calling _t_int with ((buf at 0) to int), 255
    banish buf

# ─── zero ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "zero clears one element":
    var p as ref to int is calling alloc of int
    set essence of p is 3
    calling zero of int with p, 1
    calling _t_int with (essence of p), 0
    banish p

test "zero clears many elements":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 8
    var i as int is 0
    while i < 8:
        set buf at i is i + 1
        set i is i + 1
    calling zero of int with buf, 8
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 0
    calling _t_int with (buf at 7), 0
    banish buf

test "zero clears a float buffer":
    var buf as ref to float is calling alloc_array of float with 2
    set buf at 0 is 2.5
    set buf at 1 is 3.5
    calling zero of float with buf, 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 0), 0.0
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with (buf at 1), 0.0
    banish buf

# ─── swap ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "swap exchanges two ints":
    var x as int is 1
    var y as int is 2
    calling swap of int with (sigil of x), (sigil of y)
    calling _t_int with x, 2
    calling _t_int with y, 1

test "swap is a no-op on equal values":
    var x as int is 7
    var y as int is 7
    calling swap of int with (sigil of x), (sigil of y)
    calling _t_int with x, 7
    calling _t_int with y, 7

test "swap exchanges two floats":
    var x as float is 1.5
    var y as float is 2.5
    calling swap of float with (sigil of x), (sigil of y)
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with x, 2.5
    calling ward.assert_eq_float with y, 1.5

test "swap exchanges two runes":
    var a as MemoriaPoint is with x is 1, y is 2
    var b as MemoriaPoint is with x is 3, y is 4
    calling swap of MemoriaPoint with (sigil of a), (sigil of b)
    calling _t_int with a.x, 3
    calling _t_int with a.y, 4
    calling _t_int with b.x, 1
    calling _t_int with b.y, 2

# ─── compare ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "compare returns zero for equal blocks":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 4
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 4
    set a at 0 is 5
    set b at 0 is 5
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of int with a, b, 4), 0
    banish a
    banish b

test "compare returns minus one when a is smaller":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    set a at 0 is 1
    set b at 0 is 2
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of int with a, b, 2), 0 - 1
    banish a
    banish b

test "compare returns one when a is larger":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 2
    set a at 0 is 2
    set b at 0 is 1
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of int with a, b, 2), 1
    banish a
    banish b

test "compare finds the first differing element":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 3
    set a at 0 is 1
    set a at 1 is 2
    set b at 0 is 1
    set b at 1 is 3
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of int with a, b, 3), 0 - 1
    banish a
    banish b

test "compare of zero elements is zero":
    var a as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 1
    var b as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 1
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of int with a, b, 0), 0
    banish a
    banish b

test "compare works on byte buffers":
    var a as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 2
    var b as ref to byte is calling alloc_array of byte with 2
    set a at 0 is 10
    set b at 0 is 20
    calling _t_int with (calling compare of byte with a, b, 2), 0 - 1
    banish a
    banish b

# ─── bytes_for ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "bytes_for counts int elements":
    calling _t_usize with (calling bytes_for of int with 4), 16

test "bytes_for counts byte elements":
    calling _t_usize with (calling bytes_for of byte with 7), 7

test "bytes_for counts f64 elements":
    calling _t_usize with (calling bytes_for of f64 with 3), 24

test "bytes_for counts rune elements":
    calling _t_usize with (calling bytes_for of MemoriaPoint with 5), 40

test "bytes_for of zero elements is zero":
    calling _t_usize with (calling bytes_for of int with 0), 0

# ─── composición ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

test "alloc, fill and banish compose":
    var p as ref to MemoriaPoint is calling alloc of MemoriaPoint
    calling fill of MemoriaPoint with p, (with x is 1, y is 2), 1
    calling _t_int with p->x, 1
    calling _t_int with p->y, 2
    banish p

test "resize plus defer frees exactly once":
    var buf as ref to int is calling alloc_array of int with 4
    set buf at 0 is 3
    set buf is calling resize_array of int with buf, 4, 8
    calling _t_int with (buf at 0), 3
    banish buf
